  <p class="secondary">Part III</p>
  <h1>Periodic Matter and Electronic States</h1>
  <p class="lede">A periodic one-particle Hamiltonian is organized by translation symmetry before density-functional theory enters the story. Translation symmetry supplies Bloch states, crystal momentum, bands, and Brillouin-zone equivalence; electron number and occupation then select an electronic state from that spectrum.</p>

  <h2>From molecular orbitals to an extended solid</h2>
  <p>Part II showed why one-particle states can remain useful even when electrons interact: Hartree and Hartree–Fock replace part of the many-electron structure by coupled orbital equations, while retaining clear limits on that reduction. Once one-particle states are useful, the next question is how an extended solid organizes them.</p>
  <p>A finite molecule has a finite spatial extent, so its bound one-particle states can be indexed as a discrete set of orbitals. An ideal crystal adds a different structure: translation by certain vectors reproduces the same physical environment throughout space. A <strong>Bravais lattice</strong> is the set of those vectors,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="R equals the sum from alpha equals one to d of m alpha a alpha, with integer m alpha">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;=\sum_&#123;\alpha=1&#125;^d m_\alpha\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_\alpha,\qquad m_\alpha\in\mathbb&#123;Z&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The lattice is not the crystal. A <strong>basis</strong> or <strong>motif</strong> is the physical content—atoms, ions, or another specified object—attached to each lattice point. The crystal structure is lattice plus basis. Primitive and conventional cells, space groups, and Wyckoff positions refine that description, but the electronic-state argument needs only the translation set and the contents repeated by it.</p>
  <p>Our recurring minimal model is a one-dimensional chain with spacing <math class="math-inline" aria-label="a"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Its real-space translations are <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R equals m a"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=ma</annotation></semantics></math>. It removes chemistry and dimensional complexity so that the translation labels themselves remain visible.</p>

  <h2>Translation symmetry turns repetition into a quantum label</h2>
  <p>Consider the usual one-particle Hamiltonian with a translation-invariant kinetic term and a local potential that repeats under every lattice translation. With no boundary or field that breaks the symmetry,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="V of r plus R equals V of r, therefore the commutator of H and T sub R is zero">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mspace width="1em"/><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width="1em"/><mo>[</mo><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=V(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad[\hat&#123;H&#125;,\hat&#123;T&#125;_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;]=0.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Here we define the translation operator by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T sub R acting on psi at r equals psi at r plus R"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></msub><mi>ψ</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat&#123;T&#125;_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;\psi)(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\psi(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math>. Commuting operators can be diagonalized in common symmetry sectors. Because translation preserves norm, its eigenvalues have unit magnitude; because lattice translations compose additively, their compatible eigenvalues are phases labelled by a vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="T sub R psi n k equals exponential i k dot R psi n k, equivalently psi n k at r plus R equals the same phase times psi n k at r">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" rowspacing="0.55em">
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></msub><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;T&#125;_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;=e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;,\qquad \psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <div class="translation-views">
    <section>
      <h3>Physical view</h3>
      <p>Moving by a lattice vector reaches an equivalent environment. A translation eigenstate can therefore reproduce itself up to a phase without becoming a different symmetry sector.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3>Mathematical view</h3>
      <p>The commuting unitary translations form an Abelian group. Their simultaneous eigenvalues are the phase factors <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k dot R"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <p>The vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is called <strong>crystal momentum</strong>, but its primary meaning here is the quantum label associated with lattice translations. It is not generally the electron’s free-particle mechanical momentum.</p>

  <h2>Bloch states carry a phase across identical cells</h2>
  <p>The translation statement is Bloch’s theorem. Factoring out its phase gives an equivalent cell-periodic form:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="psi n k of r equals exponential i k dot r times u n k of r, where u n k is periodic">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" rowspacing="0.55em">
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;r&#125;&#125;u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;),\qquad u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>A Bloch wave is generally <strong>not periodic</strong> even though the Hamiltonian and crystal are periodic. The factor <math class="math-inline" aria-label="u n k"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> repeats from cell to cell; the full state also accumulates the translation phase. Only when that phase is one for the chosen translation does the full Bloch state repeat exactly.</p>

  <figure class="core-diagram bloch-phase" aria-labelledby="bloch-phase-caption">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 208" role="img" aria-labelledby="bloch-phase-title bloch-phase-desc">
