      <text x="14" y="22" class="diagram-heading">cell-periodic uₖ(x)</text>
      <g class="diagram-cells">
        <rect x="24" y="82" width="96" height="70"/><rect x="132" y="82" width="96" height="70"/><rect x="240" y="82" width="96" height="70"/>
      </g>
      <g class="diagram-wave">
        <path d="M32 118 C48 88, 64 148, 80 118 S112 88, 120 118"/>
        <path d="M140 118 C156 88, 172 148, 188 118 S220 88, 228 118"/>
        <path d="M248 118 C264 88, 280 148, 296 118 S328 88, 336 118"/>
      </g>
      <g class="diagram-phasors">
        <circle cx="72" cy="52" r="20"/><line x1="72" y1="52" x2="92" y2="52"/>
        <circle cx="180" cy="52" r="20"/><line x1="180" y1="52" x2="194" y2="38"/>
        <circle cx="288" cy="52" r="20"/><line x1="288" y1="52" x2="288" y2="32"/>
      </g>
      <text x="48" y="178">cell 0</text><text x="156" y="178">cell 1</text><text x="264" y="178">cell 2</text>
      <text x="53" y="201">phase 1</text><text x="147" y="201">phase eⁱᵏᵃ</text><text x="246" y="201">phase e²ⁱᵏᵃ</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption id="bloch-phase-caption">For the chain, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="psi k of x plus a equals exponential i k a psi k of x"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_k(x+a)=e^&#123;ika&#125;\psi_k(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The local pattern repeats, while its complex phase advances from cell to cell.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="concept-check" role="note" aria-labelledby="part-iii-check-one">
    <h3 id="part-iii-check-one">Check your understanding</h3>
    <p><strong>Question.</strong> If the crystal is periodic, why is a Bloch wave not generally periodic?</p>
    <p><strong>Answer.</strong> Translation symmetry requires the full state <math class="math-inline" aria-label="psi n k"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> to return up to the phase <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k dot R"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, not necessarily to return unchanged. The factor <math class="math-inline" aria-label="u n k"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is periodic; the complete Bloch state is periodic only when the translation phase equals one.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reciprocal space removes redundant translation labels</h2>
  <p>The phase label now creates its own redundancy. Suppose a vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> leaves every lattice-translation phase unchanged:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Exponential i k plus G dot R equals exponential i k dot R when exponential i G dot R equals one, equivalently G dot R equals two pi m">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" rowspacing="0.55em">
          <mtr><mtd><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mspace width="0.7em"/><mtext>if</mtext><mspace width="0.5em"/><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^&#123;i(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;G&#125;)\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;=e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\ \text&#123;if&#125;\ e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;=1,\qquad \mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;=2\pi m.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The vectors satisfying this condition form the <strong>reciprocal lattice</strong>. Therefore <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k and bold k plus bold G"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\text&#123; and &#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> carry the same lattice-translation character. Their wavefunction representations need not be written identically, but they belong to the same class of translation labels. Only one primitive reciprocal cell is needed to choose nonredundant representatives.</p>
  <p>The <strong>first Brillouin zone</strong> is the conventional choice: the Wigner–Seitz cell of the reciprocal lattice. This geometric definition follows the physical reason for choosing it—crystal momentum repeats modulo reciprocal-lattice vectors. For the chain,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="G equals two pi n over a, and the first Brillouin zone is minus pi over a less than or equal to k less than pi over a">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.2em"/><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\frac&#123;2\pi&#125;&#123;a&#125;n,\qquad -\frac&#123;\pi&#125;&#123;a&#125;\le k&lt;\frac&#123;\pi&#125;&#123;a&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <figure class="core-diagram lattice-duality" aria-labelledby="lattice-duality-caption">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 226" role="img" aria-labelledby="lattice-duality-title lattice-duality-desc">
      <title id="lattice-duality-title">Real and reciprocal lattices of a one-dimensional chain</title>
      <desc id="lattice-duality-desc">Equally spaced real-space sites separated by a are paired with reciprocal-lattice marks separated by two pi over a. The first Brillouin zone extends from minus pi over a to pi over a.</desc>
      <text x="14" y="22" class="diagram-heading">Real space</text>
      <line x1="36" y1="68" x2="324" y2="68" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <g class="diagram-sites">
        <circle cx="60" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="120" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="180" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="240" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="300" cy="68" r="5"/>
      </g>
      <line x1="120" y1="92" x2="180" y2="92" class="diagram-measure"/>
      <line x1="120" y1="87" x2="120" y2="97" class="diagram-measure"/><line x1="180" y1="87" x2="180" y2="97" class="diagram-measure"/>
      <text x="147" y="110">a</text>
      <text x="14" y="139" class="diagram-heading">Reciprocal space</text>
      <rect x="150" y="151" width="60" height="42" class="diagram-zone"/>
      <line x1="36" y1="172" x2="324" y2="172" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <g class="diagram-ticks">
        <line x1="60" y1="162" x2="60" y2="182"/><line x1="120" y1="162" x2="120" y2="182"/><line x1="180" y1="159" x2="180" y2="185"/><line x1="240" y1="162" x2="240" y2="182"/><line x1="300" y1="162" x2="300" y2="182"/>
      </g>
      <text x="51" y="203">−2G₀</text><text x="114" y="203">−G₀</text><text x="176" y="203">0</text><text x="236" y="203">G₀</text><text x="293" y="203">2G₀</text>
      <text x="137" y="220">first BZ: [−π/a, π/a)</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption id="lattice-duality-caption">For the one-dimensional chain, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G sub zero equals two pi over a"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_0=2\pi/a</annotation></semantics></math>. Reciprocal space is not added as a numerical trick; it records which translation phases are distinct.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <dl class="paired-definitions">
    <dt><math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> — reciprocal-lattice vector</dt>
    <dd>A discrete vector defined by the real lattice; adding it does not change a translation character.</dd>
    <dt><math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> — Bloch-state label</dt>
    <dd>A generally continuous label within one Brillouin zone that identifies a translation-symmetry sector.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>Both live in reciprocal space, but they are not the same object. The combination <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k plus bold G"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> contains a Bloch-state label and a reciprocal-lattice vector; it does not make the two objects interchangeable.</p>

  <h2>At every k there is a family of eigenstates</h2>
  <p>Translation symmetry does not leave only one state at each crystal momentum. A periodic one-particle Hamiltonian generally has many eigenstates in the same translation sector:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="H psi n k equals E n of k psi n k">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;H&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;=E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The discrete index <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the <strong>band index</strong>; <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> remains the translation label. As <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> changes, the spectrum forms energy branches <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E n of bold k"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math>. Away from degeneracies one may follow an individual branch continuously; at a degeneracy, assigning separate band labels can be conventional. Additional point and space symmetries can enforce degeneracies and constrain couplings at special points or lines, but translation is what first creates the <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>-resolved organization.</p>
  <p>The one-orbital chain shows how a band can grow from repeated local states. Begin with one orbital of energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="epsilon zero"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_0</annotation></semantics></math> on each site and retain only a real nearest-neighbor matrix element <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. With the convention that this off-diagonal Hamiltonian element is <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the single band is</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="E of k equals epsilon zero plus two t cosine k a">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(k)=\varepsilon_0+2t\cos(ka).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>For this sign convention, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t less than zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> places the band minimum at <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k equals zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>; changing the sign reverses the ordering. A ring of <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sites has <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> allowed <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values in this band; they become dense in the periodic limit. This cosine is a teaching model, not a universal shape for real bands. The general definition remains the eigenvalue spectrum of the stated periodic one-particle Hamiltonian.</p>

  <figure class="core-diagram band-formation" aria-labelledby="band-formation-caption">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 214" role="img" aria-labelledby="band-formation-title band-formation-desc">
      <title id="band-formation-title">A repeated atomic level becomes a dispersive band</title>
      <desc id="band-formation-desc">One isolated energy level appears on the left, several split levels for a short chain appear in the center, and a continuous cosine-like energy band across the Brillouin zone appears on the right.</desc>
      <text x="30" y="24" class="diagram-heading">1 site</text><text x="139" y="24" class="diagram-heading">few sites</text><text x="256" y="24" class="diagram-heading">periodic chain</text>
      <line x1="22" y1="178" x2="92" y2="178" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="22" y1="178" x2="22" y2="46" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <line x1="38" y1="108" x2="78" y2="108" class="diagram-level"/><text x="47" y="98">ε₀</text>
      <line x1="126" y1="178" x2="205" y2="178" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="126" y1="178" x2="126" y2="46" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <g class="diagram-levels"><line x1="141" y1="79" x2="190" y2="79"/><line x1="141" y1="94" x2="190" y2="94"/><line x1="141" y1="110" x2="190" y2="110"/><line x1="141" y1="127" x2="190" y2="127"/><line x1="141" y1="143" x2="190" y2="143"/></g>
      <line x1="232" y1="178" x2="342" y2="178" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="232" y1="178" x2="232" y2="46" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <path d="M242 74 C268 74, 273 145, 287 145 C301 145, 306 74, 332 74" class="diagram-band"/>
      <text x="237" y="198">−π/a</text><text x="284" y="198">0</text><text x="316" y="198">π/a</text>
      <text x="8" y="43">E</text><text x="112" y="43">E</text><text x="218" y="43">E</text><text x="336" y="181">k</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption id="band-formation-caption">Equivalent local levels split when sites couple. Translation symmetry labels the resulting states by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>; in the extended limit their energies trace a band.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Occupation turns a spectrum into an electronic state</h2>
  <p>A list of bands is not yet an electronic state. The electron number and occupation rule decide which <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n bold k"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> states are filled. At zero temperature in the simplest independent-particle picture, states are filled in increasing energy up to an occupation boundary called the Fermi level. A spin-independent model may place two opposite-spin electrons in each spatial Bloch state, but that factor of two is an assumption, not a universal counting rule.</p>
  <p>If all bands below the occupation boundary are completely filled and separated from empty bands by a gap, the one-particle filling is insulating. If a band is only partially filled or crosses the Fermi level, the one-particle spectrum is metallic. A zero-gap touching case requires separate classification, and in a gapped system occupation alone does not fix the zero-temperature chemical potential to a unique point inside the gap. This is a band classification of a declared one-particle Hamiltonian. Strong interactions or symmetry breaking can produce insulating behavior that the simplest filling picture does not explain.</p>
  <p>For a metal, the reciprocal-space boundary satisfies</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="E n of bold k equals E sub F">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)=E_\mathrm&#123;F&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The solutions form the <strong>Fermi surface</strong> in three-dimensional <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> space, or a Fermi contour in two dimensions. It is not a physical crystal surface. It is the locus separating occupied and unoccupied one-particle states at the Fermi energy under the stated zero-temperature picture.</p>

  <h2>Band structure and density of states reorganize the same spectrum</h2>
  <p>A band structure retains where each eigenvalue occurs: it shows energy as a function of band index and crystal momentum. The density of states, or DOS, discards that location information and asks how many states occur in an energy interval after integrating over the Brillouin zone. A normalized conceptual form in <math class="math-inline" aria-label="d"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions is</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="D of E equals sum n integral over the Brillouin zone d to the d k over Omega B Z times delta of E minus E n of k">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>BZ</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>BZ</mi></msub></mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(E)=\sum_n\int_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;\frac&#123;d^d\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;&#123;\Omega_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;&#125;\,\delta(E-E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The delta function is a compact way to count states at energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. With this normalization, each state family counted by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> contributes one state per primitive cell when integrated over energy; spin, internal-state, and per-volume conventions add their stated factors. Peaks in the DOS indicate many states concentrated in a narrow energy range; they do not reveal which <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> regions supplied them. Conversely, a band path preserves selected momentum information but does not count the full zone.</p>

  <figure class="core-diagram band-dos" aria-labelledby="band-dos-caption">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="band-dos-title band-dos-desc">
      <title id="band-dos-title">Band dispersion and density of states are different views</title>
      <desc id="band-dos-desc">On the left, two energy bands vary with crystal momentum. On the right, a density-of-states curve counts states at each energy without preserving their momentum locations. A shared Fermi-level line crosses both views.</desc>
      <text x="75" y="22" class="diagram-heading">Eₙ(k)</text><text x="267" y="22" class="diagram-heading">D(E)</text>
      <line x1="26" y1="198" x2="190" y2="198" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="26" y1="198" x2="26" y2="38" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <path d="M38 147 C72 106, 108 106, 178 147" class="diagram-band"/><path d="M38 70 C76 98, 132 94, 178 70" class="diagram-band secondary-band"/>
      <line x1="26" y1="118" x2="334" y2="118" class="diagram-fermi"/><text x="31" y="111">E_F</text>
      <text x="34" y="218">−π/a</text><text x="104" y="218">k</text><text x="165" y="218">π/a</text><text x="10" y="38">E</text>
      <line x1="226" y1="198" x2="334" y2="198" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="226" y1="198" x2="226" y2="38" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <path d="M226 180 C250 180, 290 172, 304 153 C319 132, 249 135, 248 118 C247 101, 323 96, 307 75 C293 55, 252 48, 226 48" class="diagram-dos"/>
      <text x="324" y="218">states</text><text x="210" y="38">E</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption id="band-dos-caption">This schematic uses an illustrative two-band one-dimensional periodic model, so the interval on the left is its whole Brillouin zone and the spectrum matches at its equivalent edges. In higher dimensions a conventional band path samples only selected lines. The DOS on the right integrates over momentum and retains an energy count. Neither view is automatically an experimental spectrum: that interpretation also needs the physical excitation and probe coupling.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>A band path is not the Brillouin zone</h2>
  <p>Symmetry identifies special points and lines where degeneracies or selection constraints may be especially informative. Connecting selected high-symmetry points therefore produces a useful band plot. But the plot is a diagnostic slice through reciprocal space, not the entire reciprocal cell.</p>
  <p>A full-zone average of a quantity <math class="math-inline" aria-label="A of bold k"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math> has the distinct form</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Average A equals one over Omega B Z integral over the Brillouin zone A of k d to the d k">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>BZ</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>BZ</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle A\rangle=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\Omega_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;&#125;\int_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;A(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)d^d\mathbf&#123;k&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>A high-symmetry path cannot generally evaluate this integral and cannot prove that no off-path band extremum or Fermi pocket exists. Part V will explain how full-zone objects are represented by finite weighted sampling; DFT Research Workflow owns observable-specific convergence. Here the conceptual boundary is enough: a visualization path, an integration mesh, and a full-zone search answer different questions.</p>
  <p class="repair-links">For repair depth, see <a href={href('/theory/crystallography/')}>Crystallography</a> for cells and reciprocal geometry, <a href={href('/theory/fourier-analysis/')}>Fourier Analysis</a> for periodic representations, <a href={href('/theory/group-theory-and-symmetry/')}>Group Theory and Symmetry</a> for degeneracies and selection rules, or <a href={href('/theory/solid-state-physics/')}>Solid-State Physics</a> for broader condensed-matter context. <a href={href('/theory/brillouin-zone-sampling/')}>Brillouin-Zone Sampling</a> develops the later quadrature problem without turning a band path into an integration domain.</p>

  <p>We now know that if a one-particle Hamiltonian is periodic, its eigenstates can be chosen as Bloch states and its eigenvalues form bands whose occupation defines a one-particle electronic state. But the real electrons still interact, and Part II showed the limits of an orbital mean field. Can the interacting ground-state problem be reformulated without representing the full many-electron wavefunction?</p>
  <p>Part IV will ask how a density-based theory can make that reformulation while its auxiliary periodic one-particle Hamiltonian inherits the structure established here.</p>

